WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.260
Welcome back to SpaceX Daily, Episode 19. I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:10.000
Today is July 1st, 2026. Let's get into today's SpaceX developments.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:15.680
An 8-mile gas pipeline at Starbase targets the propellant bottleneck that has limited

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:22.720
Starship launch cadence. SpaceX filed plans to construct an 8-mile natural gas pipeline serving

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.840
the Starbase site. The line would supply fuel for Raptor engine testing and Starship propellant

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:34.720
loading. This infrastructure directly addresses ground support constraints on rapid launch turnaround.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:40.980
The project remains in permitting review. Local Texas groups posted photos of additional

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:47.420
pipeline survey markers appearing along the proposed Starbase route. That development sits inside

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:53.260
the ongoing Starship flight test campaign, where faster ground-side propellant transfer could

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:58.960
shorten the interval between landing and the next liftoff. SpaceX scheduled a Falcon 9

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:05.380
mission from Vandenberg Space Force Base ahead of the July 4th weekend. The launch window opens

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:10.960
in the coming days and may produce a visible plume across parts of the southwestern United States.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:17.900
No payload details were released in the advisory. The same Vandenberg Falcon 9 mission could appear

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:24.460
low on the western horizon for observers in northern Arizona. Viewing geometry depends on

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:31.520
exact launch time and trajectory. Local astronomy groups are circulating timing tools. Observers on

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:37.700
X are circulating predicted viewing times for the upcoming Vandenberg Falcon 9 pass over Arizona

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:44.220
and California. A separate Falcon 9 mission from Vandenberg is listed for the same holiday window.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:49.040
The advisory covers public safety notifications for coastal communities.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.280
No changes to the standard nine-minute boostback and landing profile were noted.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.100
Amateur radio operators discussed coordination frequencies for the holiday weekend launches.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:05.720
This continues the Falcon 9's record cadence, where booster reuse counts keep climbing

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:09.140
and annual launch rate limits remain the key open question.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:15.820
Elon Musk posted that the company should reduce use of the word star across future vehicle and

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:22.720
constellation names. The note follows internal discussion on branding clarity. No formal policy

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:28.440
change has been issued. A threat on naming conventions highlighted past SpaceX vehicles that

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:35.160
avoided the star prefix entirely. Observers noted that earlier company hardware often used plain

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:41.820
numeric or functional designations instead of thematic prefixes. The discussion referenced specific

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:48.240
prior examples such as early Falcon variants and test articles that carried no cosmic branding at all.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:53.840
Internal teams are still evaluating whether any naming adjustments would affect regulatory

00:02:53.840 --> 00:03:00.780
filings or public communications. The June 12 listing drew attention to valuation and personalities.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:06.860
Yet the filing also disclosed ongoing capital allocation towards starbase expansion and

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:12.920
starlink ground infrastructure. The prospectus highlighted that launch operations remain the primary

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:19.280
cash consumer. Capital expenditures listed in the document include continued work on Texas launch

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:25.540
infrastructure and additional ground stations for the satellite network. Launch cadence growth is

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:31.420
presented as the main driver of near term spending. The filing separates these operational investments

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:37.900
from any valuation metrics that received outside coverage. No specific dollar amounts for individual

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:44.180
projects beyond the general categories were broken out in the released summary. Reports indicate an

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:49.480
undisclosed starbase hardware or test milestone is expected inside the next two weeks.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:55.660
No further technical details were released. The timing aligns with ongoing booster and ship

00:03:55.660 --> 00:04:01.900
processing flows. Observers tracking vehicle movements at the site noted continued stacking and

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:08.140
tanking operations on multiple vehicles. The window coincides with standard turnaround activities

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:14.260
between flight tests. No official confirmation of the exact milestone content has been provided by

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:19.720
the company. A lieutenant from the Belton Police Department has been selected as the new chief of

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:25.600
police for the city of starbase. The appointment fills a newly created municipal position tied to

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:31.880
the growing Texas launch site. The role was established to manage public safety as the site population

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:38.140
and launch activity increase. Local reporting indicates the selected candidate brings experience

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:43.960
from municipal policing in central Texas. The position reports directly to starbase city

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:50.280
leadership rather than to space X operations. No timeline for the chief's start date was included

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:57.200
in the announcement. On the AI front, space X is offering Memphis residents half-price starlink service

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:04.100
as its local AI-related facilities expand. The discounted rate applies to households in the

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:10.840
immediate service area around the new installations. Expansion of those facilities is described as part of

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:17.280
broader infrastructure supporting compute workloads. The program is positioned as a direct benefit to

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:23.400
communities hosting the equipment. No details on the number of participating residents or the duration

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:30.600
of the discount were released. Orbital a five-month-old startup asked the FCC for authority to deploy

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:37.540
up to 100,000 data center satellites delivering 10 gigawatts of orbital compute. The application

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:43.000
outlines a constellation architecture intended to place processing hardware in low-earth orbit.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:48.520
Power generation and thermal management requirements are listed among the technical challenges

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:54.380
described in the filing. The company is separate from space X yet seeks spectrum coordination

00:05:54.380 --> 00:06:00.900
similar to existing satellite operators. Review of the request is in early stages at the commission.

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:07.400
Space X has named its planned orbital AI constellation Starmind, continuing the company's

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:14.000
established cosmic naming pattern. The designation appears in recent internal references tied to compute

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:20.760
focused orbital assets. The name follows the same prefix approach used for starlink and starship.

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:25.760
No technical specifications or deployment timeline accompanied the naming reference.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:32.000
The choice aligns with prior company practice of applying thematic labels to major programs.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:38.920
One thing worth watching a gizmodo analysis argues that simply scaling vehicle diameter does not

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:45.760
guarantee lower per kilogram costs. Once ground transport propellant flow rates and pad infrastructure

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:52.360
limits are included. The piececites historical data from earlier heavy lift concepts showing diminishing

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:58.560
returns beyond certain size thresholds. Any resolution would require demonstrated end-to-end

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:04.860
operations at the larger scale. The analysis contrasts theoretical payload gains with measured

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:10.540
logistics overhead at existing facilities. It notes that propellant transfer rates and road

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:16.520
constraints have historically offset some diameter driven advantages. The article concludes that

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:21.480
flight-proven data at full operational tempo would be needed to settle the comparison.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:27.800
From an engineering standpoint, the proposed 8-mile gas pipeline at starbase attacks the

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:33.060
logistics overhead of moving methane and oxygen from distant storage to the launch mount.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:39.460
In first principles terms, every kilogram of propellant that must be trucked or pumped across the

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:46.360
site adds both time and boil-off losses. A dedicated pipeline collapses that transport step into

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:52.420
a continuous low velocity flow whose only recurring cost is the energy to overcome friction and

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:58.840
elevation. If the raw material floor for natural gas delivery is simply the pipeline steel plus

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:04.500
the compressor power, the finished system still carries an idiot index driven by permitting,

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:11.140
trenching, and leak detection hardware. Closing that gap requires the same reuse mindset already

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:17.440
applied to boosters. Once the line is in place, its marginal cost per launch drops toward the

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:22.760
electricity and maintenance required to keep it pressurized. That shift directly supports the

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:29.060
open question of Starship refueling cadence. Faster ground-side propellant transfer removes one

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:34.820
variable from the timeline between landing and next liftoff. Letting the program test whether

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:40.500
the vehicle-side refueling hardware can actually keep pace with the new supply rate. And a quick

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:51.300
market note, SPCX closed at $170.86 down 0.8%. Before we go, watch for the exact timing on that

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:58.000
Vandenberg Falcon 9 pass and any updates on the star-based pipeline permit. That covers everything

